# Line ending policy
#
# Everything text gets stored as LF in the repository and checked out as LF on
# every platform. Without this, line endings depend on whichever git happens to
# touch the file -> there are two installs on this machine that disagreed about
# whether to apply the CRLF filter, and one reported 414 changed lines in
# gpt/model.go where the other correctly reported 70. That makes diffs
# unreadable and hides real changes inside byte noise.
#
# LF is also what the Go toolchain and gofmt are native to.
#
# NOTE: the existing history was committed with CRLF, so the first commit after
# this file lands will renormalize the files it touches. Keep that in its own
# commit if you want a clean feature diff -> see the README note below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Model weights and reference dumps are raw float32, never to be line-ending
# converted. They are gitignored too, this is belt and braces
*.safetensors binary

*.exe binary

# Training corpora are DATA, not source. Never rewrite their bytes -> the BPE
# tokenizer was trained on exactly these, and flipping CRLF to LF changes what
# the tokenizer would learn (and therefore every token id downstream of it).
# tiny_shakespeare is CRLF and tiny_wiki is LF, which is untidy but is the
# ground truth both of their vocabularies were built against
models/**/train.txt -text

# Trained model and tokenizer JSON are generated artifacts holding float
# arrays. Nothing good comes of line-ending conversion on a 4MB single line
models/**/*.json -text
